- Graceful shutdown.
- Battery-optimization guidance where relevant.
- Recovery after process death.
- Bluetooth permission revocation handling.
- Bluetooth disabled-state handling.
- Reconnection when the operating system allows it.

Do not promise uninterrupted background discovery on every Android device.

The application must remain useful when the user disables background relaying or when Android suspends its processes.

### 17. Security and Privacy Threat Model

Document and address these threats:

- Public-key substitution.
- Impersonation.
- Unauthorized contacts.
- Malicious relay devices.
- Replayed messages.
- Duplicate forwarding.
- Routing loops.
- Packet manipulation.
- Traffic analysis.
- Message leakage through logs.
- Password guessing.
- Lost or stolen devices.
- Database extraction.
- Malformed packet attacks.
- Denial of service.
- Compromised endpoints.

Implement a security policy that explains what BitCall can and cannot protect against.

End-to-end encryption cannot protect a message from an attacker who controls an endpoint while it is unlocked. Encryption also does not completely hide traffic timing, packet sizes, or the fact that devices are communicating.

Do not describe the application as unbreakable or guarantee absolute anonymity.

